{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And he offered… a bull…</b> It appears that the verse refers back to Balaam mentioned in the preceding passage, for it was he who offered the sacrifices and not Balak, since he [Balak] said to him only ״וְהָכֵן לִי בָּזֶה שִׁבְעָה פָרִים״ etc. And one needs to know why Balaam did this thing without permission from HaShem, for He commanded him only ״אַךְ אֶת הַדָּבָר״ etc. ״אוֹתוֹ תַעֲשֶׂה״. And there are some commentators who wished to say that the king [Balak] so commanded him, and they adduced support from the statement ״אֶת שִׁבְעַת הַמִּזְבְּחוֹת עָרַכְתִּי״ etc., the meaning of which indicates that he did as HaShem had told him — thus far [their words]. But these things are not logically sound, for certainly HaShem does not desire the sacrifice of the wicked, all the more so since the purpose of the offerers' intention is to do harm to His holy people. And I found that our Rabbis of blessed memory said in the Midrash (Numbers Rabbah here) the opposite, and likewise in the Gemara in tractate Sotah (47a) they said, and this is their language: Rav Yehudah said in the name of Rav, a person should always engage in Torah etc. even not for its own sake etc., for in the merit of the forty-two offerings that he offered etc. — thus far. And if their offering was by HaShem's command, who can say that the intention was not to fulfill the king's commandment — and from this Ruth descended [as a reward]." "<b>But the correct interpretation</b> in my eyes is that Balaam reasoned in his own mind that he was commanded by HaShem only regarding the actions and the words that Balaam would do in his efforts through the aspects and forces of impurity that are outside of Him, blessed be He; but as for the effort that he would exert with Him, blessed be He, as it were, this was not forbidden to him. Therefore he offered his sacrifices to HaShem — whatever would be for the honor of HaShem, he would do. And his saying <b>et the seven altars</b>, he came to see whether HaShem desired his actions or whether He was displeased with him over this as well, and he also intended to magnify his deeds, for the earlier ones [the patriarchs] would build one altar whereas he built seven, and he spoke in this manner — <b>et the seven</b> etc. — as though the divine utterance had preceded him on this matter, and he did not say ״שִׁבְעָה מִזְבְּחוֹת״ etc. And our Rabbis of blessed memory already noted this (Numbers Rabbah 20:18) — that he intended to correspond to the seven altars that the patriarchs built — thus far. And this is the verse's measure: ״The seven altars known to have been built by the patriarchs — I have arranged like them,״ and his intention in this was to diminish the greatness of the people of Israel on account of the early patriarchs who built seven altars [by claiming to match their merit]."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The citation of Sotah 47a and the Midrash frames Balaam's sacrifices positively as meritorious in some respect; the final clause about Ruth means that from these offerings ultimately descended Ruth the Moabitess.", 1 "The last clause's logic is that Balaam sought to neutralize Israel's merit from the patriarchs' altars by building corresponding altars himself."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:13:07.260826657Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}